<article
class="entry-1853 post type-post st-publish format-standard has-post-thumbnail entry category-clinic-marketing h-article-single" id="entry-1853">

<div class="h-post-format- single-layout-1">

		<header class="entry-header">

			<h1 class="entry-title">What to Say When a Patient Leaves a Bad Review (The DHA-Safe Playbook)</h1><div class="entry-meta">By <span class="posted-by vcard author">			<a title="View all posts by Abu Bakar"
				href="/our-blog/" rel="author"
				class="url fn n"				>
				<span
				class="author-name"				>
				Abu Bakar			</span>
			</a>
		</span>

		 / <span class="posted-on"><span class="published"> August 29, 2026 </span></span></div>

		</header>

	<div class="entry-content clear"
	>

		<p><img fetchpriority="high" width="700" height="367" decoding="async" src="/images/2026/08/clinic-reviews-cover-flat.png" alt="What to say when a patient leaves a bad review — the DHA-safe reply playbook, never confirm treatment details in public, move every case offline immediately" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:8px;margin-bottom:1.5em;" /></p>
<p>A one-star review lands on a Tuesday morning. &#8220;Rushed consultation, no aftercare instructions, and when I called about the swelling nobody called back.&#8221; Your first instinct is to defend the treatment. Don&#8217;t &mdash; not publicly, and not yet.</p>
<p>For a salon, a bad review is a reputation problem. For a clinic, it&#8217;s a trust problem in a category where trust is the entire sale &mdash; and the wrong public reply can turn one unhappy patient into a compliance issue on top of a marketing one.</p>
<h2>Why patients read your worst reviews first</h2>
<p>Nobody chooses an aesthetic clinic on impulse. A patient considering fillers, laser, or a skin treatment reads reviews the way she&#8217;d research a surgeon &mdash; scrolling straight past the five-star praise to see what happens when something goes wrong, and how the clinic handled it. Your reply to a bad review isn&#8217;t damage control aimed at one person. It&#8217;s evidence, read by everyone deciding whether to trust you with something on their face or body.</p>
<p>This is why review velocity and response quality carry more weight for clinics than for almost any other UAE beauty business &mdash; and why it&#8217;s step one of the trust layer we cover in our <a href="/clinic-marketing-uae-guide/">compliant clinic marketing guide</a>.</p>
<div class="gd-hook">
<p><strong>The confidentiality trap:</strong> a defensive reply that confirms what treatment a reviewer had, or discusses their specific case in public, isn&#8217;t just bad optics &mdash; it can cross into a patient confidentiality issue. The safest reply never confirms treatment details, even to correct the record.</p>
</div>
<h2>The 4-part structure, adapted for healthcare</h2>
<p>The same skeleton that works for salon reviews needs one extra rule bolted on for clinics: never confirm, deny, or discuss any specific treatment or outcome in a public reply, no matter how much you want to correct the story.</p>
<p><strong>1. Acknowledge without confirming.</strong> &#8220;I&#8217;m sorry to hear your visit didn&#8217;t meet expectations&#8221; works whether or not you can verify the details publicly &mdash; because you shouldn&#8217;t verify them publicly at all.</p>
<p><strong>2. Take the concern seriously, not personally.</strong> &#8220;Patient experience and aftercare communication matter enormously to us, and I want to look into this properly.&#8221;</p>
<p><strong>3. Move it offline immediately.</strong> A name and direct number, framed around patient care rather than damage control: &#8220;Please contact our patient care line directly so we can address this properly.&#8221;</p>
<p><strong>4. Close for the reader, not the reviewer.</strong> One line that reassures everyone watching without relitigating the specific case.</p>
<h2>Templates for the reviews every clinic gets</h2>
<h3>1. The aftercare complaint</h3>
<p><em>&#8220;Thank you for sharing this &mdash; patient aftercare and communication are something we take very seriously, and I&#8217;m sorry your experience fell short. I&#8217;d like our patient care team to follow up with you directly; please call [number] and ask for [name] so we can look into this properly.&#8221;</em></p>
<h3>2. The &#8220;it didn&#8217;t work&#8221; review</h3>
<p><em>&#8220;We appreciate you taking the time to share this. Treatment outcomes and expectations are something we discuss carefully at consultation, and I&#8217;d welcome the chance to go through your experience directly &mdash; please reach out to [number] and ask for [name].&#8221;</em></p>
<p>Note what this avoids: it never confirms which treatment, what was promised, or what happened &mdash; that conversation belongs entirely offline.</p>
<h3>3. The wait-time or front-desk complaint</h3>
<p><em>&#8220;Thank you for the honest feedback &mdash; your time matters and I&#8217;m sorry we didn&#8217;t respect it on this visit. I&#8217;ve shared this with the team, and I&#8217;d like to hear more directly: please contact us on [number], ask for [name].&#8221;</em></p>
<h3>4. The suspicious or unverifiable review</h3>
<p><em>&#8220;Thank you for reaching out. We&#8217;ve checked our patient records and can&#8217;t locate a visit matching this review &mdash; if you believe this may be in error, please contact us directly on [number] so we can look into it. We take every patient concern seriously.&#8221;</em></p>
<div class="gd-checklist">
<h3>Before you reply &mdash; the clinic-specific check</h3>
<ul>
<li>Does the reply avoid confirming or denying any specific treatment?</li>
<li>Is there zero clinical detail about the reviewer&#8217;s case, even to correct it?</li>
<li>Does it move the conversation to a private channel within the first two lines?</li>
<li>Would this reply read the same to a Dubai Health Authority (DHA) reviewer as it does to a prospective patient?</li>
<li>Has at least an hour passed since you read it, so the reply isn&#8217;t written angry?</li>
</ul>
</div>
<h2>Building review velocity without incentivising</h2>
<p>Incentivised reviews (discounts, free add-ons in exchange for a review) sit in a grey area that&#8217;s not worth the risk in a regulated category. The compliant version is simpler and just as effective: a same-day WhatsApp follow-up asking how the appointment went, with a direct review link for patients who respond positively, and a private escalation path for anyone who doesn&#8217;t. This is exactly the loop our <a href="/reviews-reputation/">reviews &amp; reputation</a> service runs, built around healthcare-appropriate scripts rather than generic templates &mdash; and it compounds directly into the ranking factors covered in <a href="/clinic-google-maps-ranking/">why clinics vanish from Google Maps searches</a>.</p>
<p>Consistency matters more than volume here. A steady trickle of recent reviews outperforms a large but stagnant total, and it&#8217;s far more forgiving of the occasional bad one &mdash; a single one-star review buried under a dozen recent five-stars barely registers. The same review engine that helps you rank also determines what a patient sees in the ten seconds before she picks up the phone, which is the same decision point covered by your <a href="/clinic-google-ads-keywords/">Google Ads landing experience</a> once she&#8217;s already clicked.</p>
<h2>Reusing patient reviews in your own marketing</h2>
<p>A review on your Google profile is one thing. Copying it into an Instagram post, an ad or a landing page is your own advertising, and healthcare advertising in Dubai is regulated by the Dubai Health Authority (DHA). We haven&#8217;t found a simple published rule we can quote, so treat this as a decision for your licensed clinic manager: check the current DHA advertising guidance, or ask the authority directly, before putting patient reviews into promotional material. Never edit a review, pair it with a treatment result image, or imply a guaranteed outcome.</p>
<p>The safe default is to let reviews do their work on Google, link to your profile from your website, and keep promotional content focused on the service experience, team and facilities. Google&#8217;s own rules on asking for reviews are on its <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/3474122" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Business Profile help page</a>: a link or QR code is fine, rewards in exchange for reviews are not.</p>
<div class="gd-cta-box">
<h3>Not sure your review replies are DHA-safe?</h3>
